              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "This guide covers all three common types of grade-to-percentage calculations. \n\nIf you want an instant calculation, **reply with your grade (e.g., \"18 out of 25\", \"B+\", or \"3.4 GPA\")** and I will convert it for you! Otherwise, you can use the calculators and formulas below.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Points to Percentage Calculator (Fraction Grades)\nIf you received a score like **17 out of 20** or **45 out of 50**, use this formula:\n\n$$\\text{Percentage (\\%)} = \\left( \\frac{\\text{Points Scored}}{\\text{Total Possible Points}} \\right) \\times 100$$\n\n* **Example:** If you scored **42 out of 50**:\n  $$\\left( \\frac{42}{50} \\right) \\times 100 = 0.84 \\times 100 = 84\\%$$\n\n---\n\n### 2. Letter Grade to Percentage Reference Table\nThis is a standard US high school and college grading scale. *Note: Some schools or professors may use slightly different ranges.*\n\n| Letter Grade | Percentage Range | Typical GPA (4.0 Scale) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **A+** | 97% \u2013 100% | 4.0 |\n| **A** | 93% \u2013 96% | 4.0 |\n| **A-** | 90% \u2013 92% | 3.7 |\n| **B+** | 87% \u2013 89% | 3.3 |\n| **B** | 83% \u2013 86% | 3.0 |\n| **B-** | 80% \u2013 82% | 2.7 |\n| **C+** | 77% \u2013 79% | 2.3 |\n| **C** | 73% \u2013 76% | 2.0 |\n| **C-** | 70% \u2013 72% | 1.7 |\n| **D+** | 67% \u2013 69% | 1.3 |\n| **D** | 63% \u2013 66% | 1.0 |\n| **D-** | 60% \u2013 62% | 0.7 |\n| **F** | Below 60% | 0.0 |\n\n---\n\n### 3. GPA to Percentage Formulas\nConverting a GPA to a percentage depends on the scale your school uses. \n\n#### A. Standard 4.0 GPA Scale (USA & International)\nThere are two common mathematical formulas used by institutions to convert a 4.0 GPA:\n\n* **Method 1: Direct Multiplication (Most Common)**\n  $$\\text{Percentage} = \\text{GPA} \\times 25$$\n  * *Example:* A **3.2 GPA** $\\times 25 =$ **$80\\%$**\n  \n* **Method 2: Weighted Linear Scale**\n  This formula is often used by colleges so that a passing GPA doesn't translate to an unrealistically low percentage.\n  $$\\text{Percentage} = (\\text{GPA} \\times 20) + 20$$\n  * *Example:* A **3.2 GPA** $\\times 20 + 20 =$ **$84\\%$**\n\n#### B. 10.0 CGPA Scale (Common in India / CBSE)\nTo convert a 10-point Indian CGPA scale to a percentage, the standard CBSE formula is:\n$$\\text{Percentage} = \\text{CGPA} \\times 9.5$$\n* *Example:* A **8.4 CGPA** $\\times 9.5 =$ **$79.8\\%$**",
